Found by the k7 native fix round (worker diagnosis from the PAR::Packer 1.064 loader source, quantified by the reviewer). PAR's Windows loader handles only plain ANSI paths below MAX_PATH (260), for its own executable and for PAR_GLOBAL_TEMP plus the tree it extracts. The bootstrap therefore hands the child the plain long path when it qualifies and otherwise the 8.3 short alias (identity-checked).

The cache file name is <asset>-<64 hex sha> (98 characters for briefing-<V>-windows-x86_64.exe) and its PAR directory adds ".par" plus about 64 characters of extracted tree. %USERPROFILE%\.claude\briefing\bin\<V>\<name>.par is already about 139 characters plus the user name, so with the tree reserve the long form never qualifies: every Windows user depends on 8.3 short names. Where the volume creates none (ReFS Dev Drives, NTFS with 8.3 generation off, the default on many non-system volumes), the binary never starts and every hook runs the Python fallback, safely and with a stated reason. Users whose profile path is outside the ANSI code page depend on 8.3 names in any case.

Also (review M3): the extracted-tree reserve (PAR_TREE_RESERVE 64) is thin; a deeper tree in a later build would show as a warm-up failure, not as "too long".

Recommendation: shorten the Windows cache layout so the long form qualifies for ordinary ASCII profiles, e.g. <config>\briefing\bin\<V>\<first 16 hex>\briefing.exe with a short PAR directory beside it, keeping the full hash in the pin file and the rehash before every run; keep the 8.3 alias as the non-ANSI path. The layout lives in the bootstrap and the POSIX starter, not in the CPAN tarball, so it does not block dzil release 0.001, but it should land before the pin commit that activates Windows binaries. Changes the cache layout of the approved design: decision Getty's. The next native k7 run records whether the windows-2022 runner creates 8.3 aliases.

Native round 2: the windows-2022 runner does create 8.3 aliases. The chain test that reaches the non-ANSI copy through its 8.3 alias passed (probe 44259077774 attempt 2), and the direct long-path run is the known failure.
Round 2 also hit MAX_PATH in the bootstrap's own lookup. The warm path looked up the cached file and its .par directory by plain paths, which Windows rejects past 260 characters (248 for creating a directory). As a result every cache hit failed on the CI temp roots.
The round 2 fix moves the file and .par lookup to the extended \\?\ path, as the cold path already did. The configuration chain <config>\briefing\bin\<V> is still created with plain paths, so a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR longer than about 248 minus \briefing\bin\<V> stops with "cannot create and bind the complete private binary cache path" and falls back.
A shorter layout, or extended paths for the whole chain, would close this together with the PAR limit above. Switching the chain to extended paths also touches the lexical versioned-cache check of the test child override.

